# 11. Checkpoint Compatibility Conversion

An important part of the implementation is checkpoint conversion.

The original pretrained/multitask checkpoint contained ViT parameter names that did not exactly match the current Hugging Face ViT implementation.

The notebook therefore performs an explicit key conversion.

Examples include:

```text
.attention.q_proj.
        ↓
.attention.attention.query.

.attention.k_proj.
        ↓
.attention.attention.key.

.attention.v_proj.
        ↓
.attention.attention.value.

.attention.o_proj.
        ↓
.attention.output.dense.

.mlp.fc1.
        ↓
.intermediate.dense.

.mlp.fc2.
        ↓
.output.dense.
```

The encoder layer prefix is also converted:

```text
vit.layers.
     ↓
vit.encoder.layer.
```

After conversion, the notebook verifies:

```text
Original checkpoint tensors : 413
Converted tensors           : 413
Current model tensors       : 413
Mapped tensors              : 413
Unmapped tensors            : 0
Missing model keys          : 0
Shape mismatches            : 0
```

The converted checkpoint is therefore loaded with:

```python
strict=True
```

This prevents silently ignoring incompatible model parameters.

---

# 12. Training Procedure

The overall training workflow is:

```text
1. Load RSVQA-LR dataset
        ↓
2. Load ViT + BERT pretrained encoders
        ↓
3. Load existing multitask checkpoint
        ↓
4. Convert checkpoint parameter names if required
        ↓
5. Verify 100% checkpoint compatibility
        ↓
6. Build train / validation DataLoaders
        ↓
7. Train multimodal multitask model
        ↓
8. Monitor validation loss
        ↓
9. Save best checkpoint
        ↓
10. Evaluate best checkpoint
        ↓
11. Perform count-focused fine-tuning
        ↓
12. Evaluate final count-finetuned model
        ↓
13. Evaluate on official test split
```

